### TITLE ###
SlicePreservesTheCollectionType
### DESCRIPTION ###
A slice takes a contiguous run of its receiver and yields the same kind -
only the length changes, and types don't track length. Slices used to type
as dynamic outright, which erased the element type and blocked every
collection refinement downstream: `xs[1:].filter(...)` fell back to the wide
`map|list` and then hinted on an append. The flag-parsing idiom
`args = args[:i] + args[i + 1:]` made this common in real scripts.
### INPUT ###
words = ["a", "b", "c"]
tail = words[1:]
for w in tail:
    split(w, ",")
rest = words[:1] + words[2:]
rest += ["d"]
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  words @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  tail @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  words @ 2:8 -> str[]
  tail @ 3:10 -> str[]
  split @ 4:5 -> dynamic
  w @ 4:11 -> str
  rest @ 5:1 -> <no-type>
  words @ 5:8 -> str[]
  words @ 5:20 -> str[]
  rest @ 6:1 -> <no-type>
  rest @ 6:1 -> list

# Symbol types
  rest (local): list
  tail (local): str[]
  w (loop): str
  words (local): str[]

# Diagnostics
  (none)
### TITLE ###
SliceOfAStrIsAStr
### DESCRIPTION ###
Strings slice too, and to a str rather than a list of characters.
### INPUT ###
name = "hello"
head = name[:2]
split(head, "e")
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  name @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  head @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  name @ 2:8 -> str
  split @ 3:1 -> dynamic
  head @ 3:7 -> str

# Symbol types
  head (local): str
  name (local): str

# Diagnostics
  (none)
### TITLE ###
SliceOfUnknownStaysDynamic
### DESCRIPTION ###
Negative case: nothing is known about what a dynamic value slices to, so the
result stays permissive rather than being pinned to a guess.
### INPUT ###
data = parse_json("[]")
part = data[1:]
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  data @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  parse_json @ 1:8 -> dynamic
  part @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  data @ 2:8 -> any

# Symbol types
  data (local): any
  part (local): dynamic

# Diagnostics
  (none)
